<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="6-gingerol analog biosynthesis (engineered)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6920" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>Ginger (Zingiber officinale) and turmeric (Curcuma longa) both belong to the ginger family (Zingiberaceae). They accumulate bioactive compounds in their rhizomes, gingerols in ginger and |FRAME: PWY-6432 &quot;curcuminoids&quot;| in turmeric |CITS: [16890967]|. Gingerols such as |FRAME: CPD-13494 6-gingerol|, |FRAME: CPD-13645 6-dehydrogingerdione| and |FRAME: CPD-13646 6-shogaol| are known to have enormous human health beneficial effects, most notably anticancer. Gingerol biosynthesis is thus a highly desired target for bioengineer. However, the biosynthesis pathway of gingerols in planta is not fully understood |CITS: [20836122], [16890967]|. Recently, a combinatory pathway was successfully constructed and expressed in E. coli |CITS: [20836122]|. The system was composed of: 1) A short chain fatty acid CoA ligase from rice that efficiently converted octanoate, supplied in the medium, to octanoyl-CoA. 2) A portion of the beta oxidation pathway from yeast that included Pox1p (acyl-CoA oxidase) and Fox2p (bifunctional with 2-enoyl-CoA hydratase and 3S-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase activities). This beta oxidation system converted octanoyl-CoA to its corresponding 3-oxoacyl-CoA, 3-oxooctanoyl-CoA. The yeast enzymes were chosen because of the bi-functional enzyme encoded by Fox2p so that only two enzymes, instead of three, are needed in the overall conversion. 3-oxoacyl-CoA thiolase was not over-expressed in this system so that over-produced 3-oxooctanoyl-CoA was not further metabolized via the rest of beta oxidation pathway. 3) A 4-coumarate CoA ligase from Lithospermum erythrorhizon that converted p-coumarate to p-coumaroyl-CoA. 4) And finally, a curcuminoid synthase (CUS) from rice that can effectively condense p-coumaroyl-CoA with 3-oxooctanoyl-CoA to form p-coumaroylhexanoylmethane, an analog of |FRAME: CPD-13645 6-dehydrogingerdione| and |FRAME: CPD-13494 6-gingerol|. The physiological role of the rice CUS in rice plants is unclear. Rice does not produce gingerols nor curcuminoids. Although several CUS have been identified from the curcuminoid-producing plant Curcuma longa |CITS: [19622354][19258320]|, it is not clear whether they have the same enzyme activity as the rice CUS in forming gingerol analogs. Gingerol synthase has not yet been identified in ginger |CITS: [16890967]|. The highest yield of p-coumaroylhexanoylmethane in the E. coli expression system was 84 mg/L.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
